  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 124 AM EST Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: More of the same dreary weather expected for the first \nhalf of the week. We remain on the periphery of upper low pressure \ncentered near the Gaspe Peninsula early this morning, with patchy \nlight rain and drizzle occurring across much of the region. This \nactivity is associated with a shortwave trough rounding the base of \nthe trough, and we'll see a series of these over the next 48-72 \nhours. As such, periods of showers will continue to plague the \nregion, particularly today and then again Tuesday into Tuesday \nnight. As has been the case, most of the precipitation will fall as \nrain, with areas AOA 2500 ft mixing with and/or changing to snow \novernight and into the early morning hours. A reinforcing shot of \ncold air Tuesday night will bring snow levels as low as 1500 ft or \nso, mainly along the international border and the northern \nAdirondacks/Greens, and this is the best chance for seeing any \naccumulating snow below 2000 ft, though even then, it'll be pretty \nminimal. The cold pool aloft and some meager instability could allow \nfor more robust showers to produce some graupel both this afternoon \nand Tuesday afternoon, along with brief moderately heavy rainfall. \nDaytime temperatures will be similar both days, mainly in the mid \n40s to low 50s, with the southern St Lawrence/Champlain Valleys \napproaching the mid 50s Tuesday afternoon under increasing W/SW \nflow. These winds will be on the breezy side Tuesday and Tuesday \nevening, particularly at summit level and on the eastern slopes of \nthe Adirondacks and central/southern Greens. Gusts to 30 mph will be \npossible in these locations, with 35 to 45+ mph atop the mountain \nsummits. Wednesday is looking to be the driest day, with shower \ncoverage to be confined to the Northeast Kingdom and mainly during \nthe morning hours. Some sunshine might break through in the \nafternoon, but it will remain seasonably cool with highs remaining \nin the 40s to around 50F.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A fairly messy weather pattern will prevail for the \nlatter half of the week. Temperatures appear likely to be about the \nsame from day-to-day with upper 30s to mid 40s during the day and \n20s to lower 30s at night. A high amplitude ridge will build across \nthe western US while a pair of shortwaves dig well into the \nsoutheastern US on Thursday. These will eventually interact with the \npersistent upper low to our north during the weekend. Depending on \nthe exact track of surface low pressure, the interaction between the \nvarious upper vorts, and which will be most intense will affect the \noutlook for Friday into Saturday. Given the broad, disorganized \nnature of the upper level features, it seems likely that the thermal \ngradient and active subtropical jet could be the deciding factors, \nwhich would favor suppressed, southern solutions. However, raw \nensemble probabilities (both statistical, AI-driven and physics-\nbased forecasts) suggest it is worth monitoring the potential for \naccumulating snow in our forecast area. We recently highlighted \npreparedness for winter weather across New England, and so it is \ngood to be ready whether or not something takes place. Given the \ntemperature forecast, this would likely be a wet snow with some rain \npotential in the valleys, and if there is much in the way of snow, \naccumulations will depend on elevation. Weather will turn cool under \nnorthwest flow heading into Sunday and Monday, with orographic, \nupslope snow showers possible.",
